.TH MINIIRCD 8 "2026-11-19" "uircd 0.2.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
miniircd \- minimal threaded IRC server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B miniircd
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B miniircd
is a small, self-contained IRC server built on the
.B uircd
engine. It serves ordinary IRC clients: registration
.RB ( NICK / USER ),
channels
.RB ( JOIN / PART / TOPIC / MODE / KICK ),
messaging
.RB ( PRIVMSG / NOTICE ),
queries
.RB ( WHO / WHOIS / NAMES / LIST ),
and operator commands
.RB ( OPER / KILL / KLINE / UNKLINE / GKILL / BANLIST ).
It runs one worker thread per core behind
.BR SO_REUSEPORT
listeners and supports optional TLS.
.PP
Settings come from a configuration file
.RB ( \-f )
and from command-line options, which override the file. With no options it
listens on port 6667 on all interfaces with no authentication required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Read a gitconfig-style configuration file. See
.B CONFIGURATION
below and the bundled
.IR miniircd.conf .
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT"
Listen port (default 6667).
.TP
.B \-6
Bind IPv6 (dual-stack).
.TP
.BI \-t " N"
Worker threads (default: number of online CPUs).
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE"
TLS certificate chain in PEM form. Supplying it enables TLS; a key
.RB ( \-k )
is then also required.
.TP
.BI \-k " FILE"
TLS private key in PEM form.
.TP
.BI \-A " FILE"
Password file for
.B PASS
and
.B OPER
authentication. Generate entries with
.BR uircdpasswd (1).
.TP
.B \-L
Require a successful login before a client may register.
.TP
.BI \-n " NAME"
Server name used in messages (default irc.local).
.TP
.BI \-N " NET"
Network name advertised in RPL_ISUPPORT (default uircd).
.TP
.BI \-m " FILE"
Message-of-the-day file.
.TP
.BI \-P " N"
Maximum concurrent connections from one source IP (default 8).
.TP
.BI \-R " SEC"
Drop a connection that has not registered within
.I SEC
seconds (default 30).
.TP
.BI \-H " SEC"
TLS handshake deadline in seconds (default 10).
.TP
.BI \-b " MS"
Accept backoff on file-descriptor exhaustion, in milliseconds (default 100).
.TP
.B \-C
Parse and validate the configuration, then exit without binding. Useful as a
configuration test.
.TP
.B \-d
Daemonize (run in the background).
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
With
.B \-f
the server reads a gitconfig-style file: bracketed
.RI [ section ]
headers,
.RI key " = " value
lines, and
.RB ' # '
or
.RB ' ; '
comments. The sections and keys are:
.RS
.TP
.B [server]
name, network, motd
.TP
.B [listen]
port, ipv6
.TP
.B [tls]
cert, key, handshake-timeout
.TP
.B [auth]
passwd, require-login, auth-deadline-ms, auth-jitter-ms, kdf-threads
.TP
.B [privacy]
host-cloak
.TP
.B [limits]
threads, accept-backoff-ms, max-per-ip, reg-timeout, max-clients,
max-channels, ping-interval, ping-timeout, flood-lines, flood-window
.RE
.PP
Times in
.B [limits]
are in seconds; the
.B [auth]
deadline and jitter are in milliseconds. An omitted key keeps the built-in
default. Command-line options override the file.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
When a password file is configured, clients log in with
.RB ' "PASS account:password" '
(or a bare
.RB ' "PASS password" '
against a shared
.RB ' * '
entry), and operators use
.RB ' "OPER name password" '.
Logins are verified off the engine lock by a small thread pool
.RB ( "[auth] kdf-threads" )
and answered at a fixed deadline whether or not a hash was checked, so response
timing does not reveal which account names exist. The deadline
.RB ( "[auth] auth-deadline-ms" )
must exceed the slowest configured scrypt.
.SH PRIVACY
By default each client's IP address is hidden from other users: the host shown
in
.B WHO
and in message prefixes is a per-run keyed hash of the real address
.RI ( <hex> .cloak).
An operator still sees real addresses in
.BR WHO ,
and bans and the log always use the real address. Set
.RB ' "[privacy] host-cloak = false" '
to show raw addresses instead. The server makes no outbound connections and
sends nothing off the machine.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Shut the server down cleanly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on a clean shutdown, and nonzero on a configuration error or a
startup failure (for example a port that cannot be bound).
.SH EXAMPLES
Run four workers requiring login against a password file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
miniircd \-p 6667 \-t 4 \-A users.passwd \-L
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate a configuration file without starting:
.PP
.RS
.nf
miniircd \-C \-f /etc/miniircd/miniircd.conf
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uircdpasswd (1)
.SH BUGS
Report security issues through the project's
.I SECURITY.md
process.
